Hospital Web Application – Penetration Testing Project

Training Organization: Networkwalks
Project Type: Authorized Hands-on Penetration Testing
Assessment: Hospital Web Application
Status: M4 – Professional Reporting

Project Overview

This project involved a practical, authorized penetration-testing assessment of a hospital web application in a controlled educational environment.

The objective was to evaluate the application's security, investigate potential vulnerabilities, assess protected document security and identify possible exposure of sensitive organizational information.

Project Milestones

M1 – Initial Access

- Web application reconnaissance and assessment.
- Investigation of authentication mechanisms.
- Retrieval of three designated PDF laboratory reports.

M2 – Data Extraction

- Examination of PDF encryption.
- Password hash analysis.
- Authorized password recovery testing.

M3 – Security Testing

- Investigation of staff salary information exposure.
- Assessment of shareholder information exposure.
- Evaluation of potential information disclosure.

M4 – Professional Reporting

- Documentation of assessment methodology.
- Recording of security observations and validated findings.
- Development of remediation recommendations.

Tools and Technologies

- Kali Linux
- Burp Suite
- Nmap
- Gobuster
- Nikto
- John the Ripper
- Linux command-line utilities

Skills Demonstrated

- Web application reconnaissance
- Authentication security assessment
- SQL injection investigation
- HTTP request and response analysis
- PDF encryption assessment
- Password hash analysis
- Information disclosure assessment
- Security documentation and reporting

Conclusion

This project provided valuable hands-on experience applying penetration-testing methodologies to a simulated hospital web application.

It strengthened my ability to investigate security weaknesses, work with industry-standard tools, analyse technical evidence and document security findings professionally.

The completion of this project represents another important step in my development towards becoming a skilled penetration tester.

